{:edb/db-before-t 2805 :edb/db-after-t 2806 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536118 50 #inst "2026-10-09T15:29:11.336Z" 13194139536118 true] [17592186082971 1100 "For the parametric curve\n$x=\\cos{t}, \\quad y=\\sin{t}, \\qquad 0\\leq t<2\\pi$, calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/798/Source/Images/0f8bfc82b98e63e586e9d2e96d1acc5d.png)" 13194139536118 false] [17592186082971 1100 "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\cos{t}, \\quad y=\\sin{t}, \\qquad  0\\leq t<2\\pi,\n$$\n\n calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14077511/assets/2ec95dd061fa6b7f09c9f899a1f6483b4ea7d5127372ddd6eefb74d6ed10c14c.png)" 13194139536118 true] [17592186082971 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y$, we get\n\n$$\nx'(t) = -\\sin{t}, \\qquad y'(t)=\\cos{t}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\cos t}{-\\sin t} = -\\cot t\n$$" 13194139536118 false] [17592186082971 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t) = -\\sin{t}, \\qquad y'(t)=\\cos{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\cos t}{-\\sin t} = -\\cot t.\n$$" 13194139536118 true] [17592186082972 1100 "For the parametric curve\n$x=te^t, \\quad y=e^{-t}, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty$,\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1$." 13194139536118 false] [17592186082972 1100 "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=te^t, \\quad y=e^{-t}, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" 13194139536118 true] [17592186082972 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y$, we get\n\n$$\nx'(t) = e^t + te^t = (1+t)e^t, \\qquad y'(t)=-e^{-t}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x} & = \\frac{- {e}^{- t}}{( 1 + t ) {e}^{t}} \\\\  & = - \\frac{1}{( 1 + t ) {e}^{2 t}} .\\end{array}$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1$, the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}{\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x} |}_{t = 1} & = - \\frac{1}{{e}^{2 ( 1 )} ( 1 + 1 )} \\\\  & = - \\frac{1}{2 {e}^{2}} .\\end{array}$$" 13194139536118 false] [17592186082972 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t) = e^t + te^t = (1+t)e^t, \\qquad y'(t)=-e^{-t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{-{e}^{-t}}{(1+t){e}^{t}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(1+t){e}^{2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2(1)}(1+1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536118 true] [17592186083155 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384405 13194139536118 true] [17592186083155 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384416 13194139536118 true] [17592186083155 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384428 13194139536118 true] [17592186083155 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384435 13194139536118 true] [17592186083155 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384442 13194139536118 true] [17592186083158 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384412 13194139536118 true] [17592186083158 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384419 13194139536118 true] [17592186083158 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384422 13194139536118 true] [17592186083158 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384425 13194139536118 true] [17592186083825 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536118 true] [17592186083825 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14077511/assets/1739317bf1b1c567f74dccf5b24a1b1dde8eb101d20ceec9652f604dc6f84543.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" 13194139536118 true] [17592186083825 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/798/Source/Images/q-6808.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve\n$x = 4\\cos t, y = 3\\sin 2t, 0 \\le t < 2\\pi $,\nshown above, calculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t = \\frac{\\pi }{6}$." 13194139536118 false] [17592186083825 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /6} & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384405 1017 #edb/ref 17592186384406 13194139536118 true] [17592186384405 1089 "q-20990" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384405 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536118 true] [17592186384405 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14077511/assets/5855e92b04ffbf8447d32c7edb190cd16046a13e8808e6ffaade1f59e414c869.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=2t{e}^{t},\\,y={t}^{2}+2t,\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=1.$" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384405 1113 #uuid "4c14e172-0d42-58ef-86b2-05fb93de9caa" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384405 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}(t+1),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t+2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2t+2}{2{e}^{t}(t+1)}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384406 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384407 13194139536118 true] [17592186384406 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384408 13194139536118 true] [17592186384406 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384409 13194139536118 true] [17592186384406 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384410 13194139536118 true] [17592186384406 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384411 13194139536118 true] [17592186384406 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536118 true] [17592186384406 1041 "selection" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384406 1104 #edb/ref 17592186384411 13194139536118 true] [17592186384406 1110 #uuid "0c9027d3-9084-52ea-a1c8-49b25edddbc3" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384407 1034 "\\frac{1}{2e}" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384407 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536118 true] [17592186384407 1111 #uuid "7479fc56-b1d6-5832-8780-701555ad4f83" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384408 1034 "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384408 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536118 true] [17592186384408 1111 #uuid "3c264130-b076-5126-b15d-095f695b8dcc" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384409 1034 "\\frac{1}{3e}" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384409 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536118 true] [17592186384409 1111 #uuid "cf00900f-0e76-5f82-90fe-345951cc4279" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384410 1034 "\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384410 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536118 true] [17592186384410 1111 #uuid "88916111-09cc-5353-91e0-95968d526718" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384411 1034 "\\frac{1}{e}" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384411 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536118 true] [17592186384411 1111 #uuid "9b43152e-5dab-5d2e-9162-53a203de03d9" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384412 1017 #edb/ref 17592186384413 13194139536118 true] [17592186384412 1089 "q-348304" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384412 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536118 true] [17592186384412 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14077511/assets/7ae65c873838ede4cce89c953ff2654fa1e58e65fdc87896c8ba85bbf947be20.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384412 1113 #uuid "7ae411a5-1447-590c-b31c-0b3da5778742" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384412 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /4} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384413 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384414 13194139536118 true] [17592186384413 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384415 13194139536118 true] [17592186384413 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536118 true] [17592186384413 1041 "field-1" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384413 1104 #edb/ref 17592186384414 13194139536118 true] [17592186384413 1110 #uuid "73fff2be-be7b-5566-b79e-3d5ec3045bcb" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384414 1034 "1" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384414 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536118 true] [17592186384414 1111 #uuid "952e3d08-f150-517b-a16b-e760cbc4bca2" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384415 1034 "-1" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384415 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536118 true] [17592186384415 1111 #uuid "138c4bfd-92b3-5bcb-b773-aee7364dc940" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384416 1017 #edb/ref 17592186384417 13194139536118 true] [17592186384416 1089 "q-353346" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384416 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536118 true] [17592186384416 1100 "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{t},\\,y=8t-3,\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=4.$\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,4}=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384416 1113 #uuid "6a75626e-e33d-522c-9cd4-0c52b5a43afb" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384416 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{8}{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}})}=16\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=4$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,4} & =16\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =16(2) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384417 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384418 13194139536118 true] [17592186384417 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536118 true] [17592186384417 1041 "field-1" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384417 1104 #edb/ref 17592186384418 13194139536118 true] [17592186384417 1110 #uuid "31ea4f65-6086-59c4-80ad-2f87661f2742" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384418 1034 "32" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384418 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536118 true] [17592186384418 1111 #uuid "9fe12c1a-816d-5bf3-b6e6-5e2c36ca5044" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384419 1017 #edb/ref 17592186384420 13194139536118 true] [17592186384419 1089 "q-353350" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384419 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536118 true] [17592186384419 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14077511/assets/abab926e465522d30b236577bf8a1654502807b864c9fed8a4cebb84efffd3a2.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384419 1113 #uuid "9226c1a9-368c-5df7-b150-42ad7ca22abf" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384419 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /4} & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384420 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384421 13194139536118 true] [17592186384420 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536118 true] [17592186384420 1041 "field-1" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384420 1104 #edb/ref 17592186384421 13194139536118 true] [17592186384420 1110 #uuid "df771847-66bc-58d7-a898-aae8c817eab2" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384421 1034 "-\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384421 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536118 true] [17592186384421 1111 #uuid "181c81c7-97db-5341-a50d-fc0697d10adf" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384422 1017 #edb/ref 17592186384423 13194139536118 true] [17592186384422 1089 "q-353351" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384422 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536118 true] [17592186384422 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14077511/assets/35c62ccf48c4d0a49729da04b443cb94d112e673e0c24e7784d22f48eaabf8f7.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384422 1113 #uuid "4242fbb0-335c-5f3d-98d5-a78820c5ed85" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384422 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /3} & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384423 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384424 13194139536118 true] [17592186384423 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536118 true] [17592186384423 1041 "field-1" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384423 1104 #edb/ref 17592186384424 13194139536118 true] [17592186384423 1110 #uuid "8d89fe0c-fcc2-58fa-a0d7-3c915d13cefd" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384424 1034 "\\frac{8}{3\\sqrt{3}}" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384424 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536118 true] [17592186384424 1111 #uuid "d7dfe366-6105-5e10-aefd-217ec1baad4f" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384425 1017 #edb/ref 17592186384426 13194139536118 true] [17592186384425 1089 "q-363490" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384425 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139536118 true] [17592186384425 1100 "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={e}^{3/t},\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}},\\,0<t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384425 1113 #uuid "cb37c8f1-fe89-5b06-b034-120e935850f9" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384425 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{3/t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}})}{(-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{3/t})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{3/t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3({1}^{2}+4)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3(5)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15\\sqrt{5}\\,{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{75{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384426 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384427 13194139536118 true] [17592186384426 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536118 true] [17592186384426 1041 "field-1" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384426 1104 #edb/ref 17592186384427 13194139536118 true] [17592186384426 1110 #uuid "2be19854-b6fc-5df7-9700-ba7007a4872f" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384427 1034 "\\frac{1}{15e^3\\sqrt{5}}" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384427 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536118 true] [17592186384427 1111 #uuid "4ac5c850-68c2-59e8-a44d-fefa180efc41" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384428 1017 #edb/ref 17592186384429 13194139536118 true] [17592186384428 1089 "q-6810" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384428 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536118 true] [17592186384428 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14077511/assets/4969bf3cea8290c76e79419ed0160a8c1f92d7415250305df56eb8128ad15cf4.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx={t}^{4}+t-1,\\,y={t}^{3},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=-1.$" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384428 1113 #uuid "10c1d858-e83c-53dc-8a2e-e0da605f9c99" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384428 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4{t}^{3}+1,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{4{t}^{3}+1}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}}{4(-1)^{3}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(-4)+1} \\\\  & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384429 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384430 13194139536118 true] [17592186384429 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384431 13194139536118 true] [17592186384429 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384432 13194139536118 true] [17592186384429 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384433 13194139536118 true] [17592186384429 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384434 13194139536118 true] [17592186384429 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536118 true] [17592186384429 1041 "selection" 13194139536118 true] [17592186384429 1104 #edb/ref 17592186384433 13194139536118 true] [17592186384429 1110 #uuid "f342eb28-10